rowed it by age so  because if you sort of expand the age  range you hit different historical  periods so there are people who were GI  is perhaps during the Korean War who met  their spouses that way and so we wanted  to constrain the historical period  because there's a lot of events that  occur that might drive these things so  we wanted to be able to tell a clean  story okay so what time period are you  looking at well so these folks were 17  or 18 rather 235 and they had to be  married within the last ten years okay  all right so what roles do age and  citizenship play in the equation yeah so  what we found is that men and women so a  US citizen man and we only looked at  different sex marriages in this paper a  US citizen man who marries a non citizen  woman abroad either before they might  before the woman migrates or in the same  year those couples have an average age  gap about seven years so the man is  about seven years on average older than  the woman okay when we look at couples  so non citizen women that are married to  citizen men but married after the woman  migrated that gap is three years and  then we look at non citizen women who  marry someone who's a non citizen man in  the US the gap is one year so there's a  the gap is really quite large and if you  look at from the point of view of men so  non citizen men who are married to US  citizen women abroad the gap is three  years or sorry the gap is less than one  year so there's only husbands are only a  little bit older than women and then if  you look at non citizen women who are  married to citizen men but married in  the  us after a year or more of arrival the  gap is two years and so again so even  even for non-citizen men they're sort of  they have to marry a woman not older  than them but the age gap is smaller so  there's it looks like both men and women  are doing some kind of trade but the the  gap is largest for the women that are  traveling from abroad so what are you  mentioned the word trade what is being  traded right so we argue that again  previous research really looks at how  what happens after people arrive to the  u.s.  these are women by definition and men  who do not have citizenship status okay  when you marry someone who is a US  citizen it is much easier to eventually  become you know a permanent resident and  then that's a path to citizenship right  so we're arguing you know even though we  can't measure it directly that women who  marry a US citizen prior to coming to  the u.s. that this is a faster route to  becoming a US citizen so you know you  could imagine that if you come to the US  okay so let's say you marry someone from  abroad and that person is also not a US  citizen right so you're both in the same  boat to speak right so anyhow so that's  that's what we can capture we can't you  know it's it's essentially data that's  like the US Census so we're not talking  to individuals we can't measure their  actual motivations all we can do is look  at the timing of these events but we can  say this it's nationally representative  so what we're saying is true for the  entire US population okay and in terms  of people coming in from other countries  did you track that at all so where  they're coming yeah so we looked at the  the region of the world we tried to do  this by country too but the the numbers  of people get high  small but we found very big variations  and so the patterns we found are  actually consistent with some of the  media accounts so foreign-born Brides  who are who meet their spouses before  coming to the US or in the same year as  coming to the u.s. ones from Southeast  Asia the age gap is ten years okay so  they're marrying men that are on average  ten years older Russia is the next  highest region and that's about eight  years okay and so compare that to  someone who was say from Canada who is  who might meet you know their spouse  abroad and that gap is two or three  years so that's much like the gender gap  i