s and  with there have been documentary films  following folks who travel abroad to  meet to meet people so we are surprised  actually to find that's a really big  difference  interesting so are you going to be  looking at the same group of people down  the road in terms of divorce rates for  instance or any is there going to be any  kind of a follow up that's a great  question for some datasets there are  large longitudinal components where they  follow the same group of people over  time and for our book that's what we're  doing that we have a data set with lots  of young people and we can follow them  over time when static collected by other  folks but with US census kinds of data  you can't do that it's not longitudinal  so it's just a snapshot of the u.s.  population at one point in time okay so  we could look at you know in 10 or 20  years whether the regional patterns hold  or whether the age gap shrinks or grows  but we can't say anything specifically  about these people I understand okay and  then so what do you conclude in your  paper so we conclude that there is  evidence especially for non citizen  women and citizen men that that meet a  bride that there is some kind of  exchange between a more youthful  partner so youthful bride for men that  are citizen citizens  so I forgot to say this that also these  men tend to be older than the typical  men who are married they tend to be more  likely to be on their second or higher  order marriage so it's a very distinct  population that that travels or maybe  they don't travel but they are abroad  when they meet their partner okay all  right this has been fascinating thank  you so much for being here with us today  thank you for speaking with me for more  information about professor Cao and her  research please visit our website at  macmillan report jled you be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  [Music]

